l RHCL Helm charts (branch A) | Implementation (branch) | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E005 | Kuadrant on xKS via standalone Authorino + Limitador (branch B) | Implementation (branch) | MaaS | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E005 | Auth enforcement via direct Envoy ext_authz integration (branch C) | Implementation (branch) | MaaS | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | MaaS Dev Preview deployment validation on CKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | MaaS Dev Preview deployment validation on AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | MaaS observability: Perses dashboards and CKS Prometheus integration | Implementation | MaaS / xKS Platform | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | MaaS observability: structured logging and CKS Loki integration | Implementation | MaaS / xKS Platform | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | MaaS observability: distributed tracing via OpenTelemetry | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | MaaS on xKS documentation: user identity flow and known limitations | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n\nMain plan: 10 epics (E001-E004, E006-E011; the E005 slot is reserved for the branch-only Kuadrant deployment epics). Branch epics: 3 conditional alternatives attached to the E001 investigation. Total epic count: 13.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support\"] --> E006\n    E002[\"E002 Investigation: MaaS 3.4 baseline changeability\"] --> E003\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003[\"E003 maas-controller group claim mapping\"] --> E006\n    E004[\"E004 maas-api xKS adaptation\"] --> E006\n    E004 --> E008\n    E006[\"E006 CKS deployment validation\"] --> E007\n    E006 --> E008\n    E006 --> E009\n    E006 --> E010\n    E003 --> E011\n    E004 --> E011\n    E006 --> E011\n    E007[\"E007 AKS deployment validation\"] --> E011\n    E008[\"E008 metrics dashboards\"] --> E011\n    E009[\"E009 structured logging\"] --> E011\n    E010[\"E010 distributed tracing\"] --> E011\n```\n\nBranch epics (not main-plan nodes): `BRANCH-A-E005`, `BRANCH-B-E005`, `BRANCH-C-E005` each depend on and are gated by E001. They are conditional alternatives — exactly one is executed based on the E001 outcome — and are attached to the E001 investigation as a plan document, not wired into the main-plan DAG.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nE001 (RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and deployment mechanism) has three bounded outcomes, matching the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options:\n\n- **Branch A** — RHCL officially supports xKS with Helm charts: deploy Kuadrant via official RHCL Helm charts (owner: RHCL / OpenShift team).\n- **Branch B** — No official RHCL xKS support: deploy standalone Authorino + Limitador via upstream Helm (owner: MaaS team).\n- **Branch C** — Kuadrant cannot be installed on xKS at all: integrate auth enforcement directly via Envoy ext_authz, bypassing Kuadrant (owner: MaaS team; may require reworking MaaS AuthPolicy usage at the gateway).\n\nEach branch epic sets `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8003-E001` (also in its `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact`. Main-plan epics that need the outcome (E006) depend on the E001 investigation itself, never on a branch epic.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | Whether the Tenant CRD is extensible in the 3.4 GA baseline determines whether the controller epic is a code change or a documentation fallback. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | Whether maas-api can be instrumented without conflicting with the 3.4 GA freeze determines the metrics scope (in-place vs post-3.4 patch vs deferred). |\n| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | The selected Kuadrant deployment mechanism (branch A/B/C) determines what the CKS validation deploys and validates. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Rule 3 / conditional branches | Three bounded deployment outcomes; exactly one branch epic is realized per the investigation result. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 12/13 (build-against artifact) | CKS validation deploys and exercises the adapted maas-controller and its Helm chart. |\n| E004 → E006 | Rule 12/13 (build-against artifact) | CKS validation deploys and exercises the adapted maas-api/payload-processing and their Helm charts. |\n| E006 → E007 | Validation sequencing (strategy validation approach) | The strategy explicitly sequences CKS first, then AKS; AKS validation incorporates the CKS engineering delta and platform-specific GatewayClass options (envoy-gateway / azure-application-gateway). |\n| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | Perses dashboards are built against the maas-api `/metrics` instrumentation (metric names and labels). |\n| E006 → E008 | Serial (AC gate) | Dashboard acceptance requires live data from the CKS Prometheus instance scraping a deployed MaaS. |\n| E006 → E009 | Serial (AC gate) | Logging acceptance requires a deployed MaaS emitting authentication, rate-limit, and validation events into CKS Grafana Loki. |\n| E006 → E010 | Serial (AC gate) | Tracing acceptance requires the deployed request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend). |\n| E003, E004, E006, E007, E008, E009, E010 → E011 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built and the limitations found across all implementation work. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| HLR-1: MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E006 |\n| HLR-2: MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E007 |\n| HLR-3: Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 (Limitador deployment), E006 (validation) |\n| HLR-4: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity | P0 | E004 (configuration), E006 (validation) |\n| HLR-5: API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E004 (confirmation of OIDC-agnostic path), E006 (validation) |\n| HLR-6: Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-7: RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001 (+ BRANCH-A/B/C-E005) |\n| HLR-8: Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E004 (instrumentation), E008 (dashboards / Prometheus integration) |\n| HLR-9: Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E003 (mapping), E006 (validation) |\n| HLR-10: Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E009 |\n| HLR-11: Known limitations documented | P1 | E011 (fed by E006/E007 findings) |\n| HLR-12: User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E011 |\n| HLR-13: Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E010 |\n\nAll 13 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mappings\n\nCanonical component names are taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical epic component | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kuadrant (Authorino), Kuadrant (Limitador) | Model as a Service | No canonical Kuadrant component exists; the Kuadrant integration is documented inside the MaaS architecture context (`models-as-a-service.md`, `PLATFORM.md`) and exists solely to serve MaaS auth/rate-limiting on xKS. |\n| Gateway API | (folded; no separate epic) | No canonical Gateway API component. The change is Helm chart values (configurable gateway class name instead of `openshift-default`) folded into E003/E004, plus platform validation inside E006/E007. The Gateway API implementation on CKS (Istio) is a confirmed precondition per the strategy's dependency table, not new component work. |\n| Observability (Prometheus, Perses) | Monitoring | Closest canonical name for the Prometheus/Perses/Loki/Tempo stack; `odh-observability` exists in architecture context. |\n| Documentation deliverables | Documentation | Canonical component for docs-authoring work. |\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Schedule risk (external, not an AC):** MaaS 3.4 GA stable build timing vs the Summit (May 2026) announcement. Mitigations per strategy: obtain RC build by April 27, run CKS validation in parallel, maintain a RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo. Captured as a risk on E006/E007, not as acceptance criteria.\n2. **Scope trap — API key cache:** the risk table proposes an in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5 min) for validated API key hashes, but the strategy's failure-mode acceptance criteria expect API key validation to fail on PostgreSQL unavailability (cache-miss scenario). The cache is a mitigation option, not an HLR; the implementing team decides. Flagged to avoid silent scope growth in E004.\n3. **Process gates pending human review:** ODH/RHOAI build onboarding (Helm chart build pipeline), license validation, and documentation support are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy's prerequisites table. No onboarding-chain epics were generated because both affected components (Model as a Service, Monitoring) already exist in architecture context; if build onboarding is later required for the Helm charts, it should be added as `konflux-onboarding`/`license-validation` epics.\n4. **Docs priority exception:** E011 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Blocker` → Critical → P0) even though its HLRs are P1/P2. It does not push priority upstream.\n5. **Potential scope gap:** the Impacted Teams table assigns xKS Platform \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\" (RHAII installer integration), but there is no affected-components row and no HLR for it; it is not decomposed into an epic. Product should confirm whether Dev Preview requires installer integration or standalone Helm deployment suffices.\n6. **Open questions resolved without epics** (implementation details / schedule questions, per the two-way distinction): shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL (chart value decision, AC on E004); OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages (implementation detail, E004); latest stable MaaS 3.4 build date (PM schedule question — tracked in E002's findings and warning 1); CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility (validated as part of E006/E008/E009 acceptance, assumption confirmed by xKS Platform).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applied as a structural split. The external dependency with uncertain timing (MaaS 3.4 GA stable build) gates only the validation epics; all preparatory work (investigations, controller/api adaptation, Helm packaging, dashboards) is already schedulable in parallel and delivers risk-reduction value on an RC build. The strategy's own mitigation (RC build by April 27, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) is a schedule-risk handling, not a Tier 1/Tier 2 feature split.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":49076,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b20b78a95a65165e","agent_id":"a61389ab3fb18a498","session_id":"1e4ce44d-83b4-4c13-8abf-b56141fd026a","uuid":"f816e875-9339-4e34-992f-d270ada5a5f0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:09:36.432Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
