{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-d5a0dbee-5b9f-4715-bdc0-06ee9d788650","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-936f1bbf7c3b729d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-t93iw4co/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E004.md","content":"# maas-api: xKS adaptation, external OIDC configuration, metrics instrumentation, Helm charts\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic adapts maas-api (and the payload-processing BBR ext_proc server) for non-OpenShift Kubernetes: external OIDC authentication configuration replacing OpenShift's KubernetesTokenReview identity, removal of OpenShift-specific assumptions (cert-manager TLS instead of OpenShift service-CA, configurable gateway class), Prometheus metrics instrumentation for the observability value proposition, and Helm chart packaging for Helm-only xKS installation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Update the MaaS AuthPolicy deployment configuration to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, driven by the existing Tenant CRD OIDC fields (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`) — a configuration change, not an identity-model change.\n- Confirm the API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*`, maas-api callback validation via Authorino HTTP callback) remains functional and OIDC-agnostic; no changes expected.\n- Remove OpenShift-specific assumptions in deployment configuration: cert-manager-issued TLS certificates instead of OpenShift service-CA auto-generated certificates; configurable gateway class name.\n- Add Prometheus metrics instrumentation to maas-api (`/metrics` endpoint): token usage, rate limiting enforcement, API key lifecycle (creation, validation, revocation), and inference request latency, exposed as Prometheus-compatible endpoints.\n- Package maas-api and payload-processing as Helm charts with configurable values for: gateway class name, OIDC issuer configuration (issuer URL, client ID, audience, group claim path), TLS certificate source (cert-manager), and PostgreSQL connection (shared with other RHAII components or dedicated — decision captured as a chart value).\n- Optional mitigation (team decision, not required by HLRs): in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5 min) for validated API key hashes to tolerate PostgreSQL unavailability.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a CKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant and Gateway API (Istio) installed, when MaaS is deployed via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then the maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n- Given an OIDC provider outage, when inference requests are made, then requests using valid API keys continue to succeed (API key validation is OIDC-independent) while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error, measured by response status and error body.\n- Given a PostgreSQL database outage, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n- Prometheus metrics are exposed by maas-api including `maas_api_key_created_total`, `maas_inference_requests_total{user, model, subscription}`, and `maas_rate_limit_enforced_total{user, model, exceeded_quota}` (instrumentation; dashboard integration is validated separately).\n- RHOAI MaaS GA behavior is unchanged where xKS configuration is not in effect (additive/config-driven backwards compatibility).\n- If maas-api instrumentation conflicts with the MaaS 3.4 GA freeze (per the baseline changeability investigation), the metrics are delivered as a post-3.4 patch or deferred with PM sign-off, and that outcome is documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR [P0]: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n- HLR [P0]: API key authentication (`sk-oai-*` flow) functional on xKS.\n- HLR [P0]: Observability metrics functional — instrumentation portion (token usage, rate limiting enforcement, API key lifecycle events).\n\n## Risks\n\n- Observability instrumentation may require a post-3.4 patch if maas-api is frozen (strategy assumption \"Needs validation\"); approach determined by the baseline changeability investigation.\n- Helm chart build pipeline onboarding is pending human review per the strategy's prerequisites table.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":67205,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b20b78a95a65165e","agent_id":"a61389ab3fb18a498","session_id":"1e4ce44d-83b4-4c13-8abf-b56141fd026a","uuid":"57c42f7b-2828-4078-b7e6-2ed1afbc4f9b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:10:41.070Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
