{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-23d71b78-67d3-4c20-9b18-748412c4d562","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-85c74abd2c1d8d97","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-t93iw4co/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E005.md","content":"# Auth enforcement via direct Envoy ext_authz integration (branch C)\n\n## Description\n\nThis branch epic implements the strategy's contingency: when neither official RHCL Helm charts nor standalone Kuadrant deployment is viable on xKS, integrate authentication and rate-limiting enforcement directly at the gateway via Envoy's ext_authz mechanism, bypassing Kuadrant. This is a deeper architectural change that may require reworking how MaaS wires its AuthPolicy and TokenRateLimitPolicy enforcement into the gateway data plane.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Design and implement Envoy ext_authz-based authentication enforcement for the MaaS gateway on xKS, validating OIDC JWTs (external identity provider) and MaaS API keys (`sk-oai-*` validation callback path).\n- Implement rate-limiting enforcement equivalent to the MaaS token rate limiting tiers at the gateway or an equivalent enforcement point.\n- Rework the MaaS gateway configuration (Istio GatewayClass on CKS) to route authentication and rate-limit decisions through the ext_authz path instead of Kuadrant-generated policies.\n- Preserve the MaaS failure-mode behavior: API key validation remains independent of the OIDC provider; rate-limiting unavailability denies requests with HTTP 503.\n- Document the architectural deviation from the Kuadrant-based design and its long-term viability implications for product leadership.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- An inference request with a valid OIDC JWT or valid MaaS API key is allowed, and a request with invalid credentials is rejected, measured by response status codes through the gateway.\n- Given a configured token rate limit, when a user exceeds the limit, then requests are rejected with HTTP 429, measured by monitoring response status codes.\n- Given the rate-limiting enforcement point is unavailable, when a user makes an inference request, then the request is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and gateway logs.\n- A design document describes the ext_authz-based enforcement architecture and its deviations from the Kuadrant-based MaaS architecture, reviewed by the MaaS team.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR [P0]: Token rate limiting functional on xKS (enforcement via the contingency path).\n- HLR [P0]: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity (validation via the contingency path).\n\n## Gate Conditions\n\nExecuted only if the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation selects outcome C (no viable Kuadrant deployment on xKS). If no auth enforcement path is viable at all, the MaaS xKS Dev Preview scope must be re-negotiated with product leadership.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72390,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b20b78a95a65165e","agent_id":"a61389ab3fb18a498","session_id":"1e4ce44d-83b4-4c13-8abf-b56141fd026a","uuid":"2f4c2135-7d17-47fe-8f6b-eaa88da893e1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:11:33.449Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
