esolved by E001 before it starts). The rationales articulate a defensible distinction — the gate rewrites what E003/E004 themselves build, while E006's own work (deploy, validate, report) is invariant to which mechanism it deploys — but the two treatments of structurally similar gates should be reconciled or the distinction stated explicitly in the rubric sense.\n59\t\n60\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n61\tEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then ACs with measurement methods taken from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs: E011 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review sign-off AC; the konflux build-pipeline AC is N/A (no konflux chain); no replacement epics exist, and E003/E004 nonetheless carry backwards-compatibility ACs (\"RHOAI MaaS GA behavior unchanged\") matching the strategy's compatibility NFR. No soft ACs: the external schedule constraint (Summit/MaaS 3.4 build timing) is correctly filed as a Risk on E006 and a Health Warning in the summary, not as an AC. No draft-ID references in any epic body (verified by scan — epic prose refers to \"the baseline changeability investigation\" etc., never \"E002\"). One minor issue: E004's first four ACs are system-level runtime behaviors on a deployed CKS instance (pod readiness, API key create-and-use, OIDC-outage and PostgreSQL-outage behavior) that duplicate E006's validation ACs verbatim and cannot be verified within E004's own deliverables (charts, configuration, instrumentation) given its only dependency is E002. They should be scoped to E004's build artifacts (e.g., chart renders with the enumerated values; metrics endpoint exposes the named metrics) with the runtime behaviors left to E006.\n62\t\n63\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n64\tCore scope is fully accounted for: all nine happy-path acceptance criteria, three of four failure-mode criteria, the three bounded Kuadrant deployment options (branches A/B/C match the strategy's priority-ordered options exactly), the scope-reduction table (operator exclusion re