view) with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, aligned with the MaaS AuthPolicy configuration.\n43\t- Confirm Limitador's TokenRateLimitPolicy functionality on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (no code changes expected).\n44\t- Enable structured logging for authentication decisions (Authorino) and rate limit enforcement (Limitador), formatted for aggregation in CKS Grafana Loki.\n45\t- Document the unsupported-path implications (no RHCL official support) for product leadership visibility, per the strategy's strategic-dependency risk.\n46\t\n47\t## Acceptance Criteria\n48\t\n49\t- Standalone Authorino and Limitador are installed via Helm on a CKS cluster and all pods are running and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks.\n50\t- Authorino validates an OIDC JWT against the configured external identity provider and rejects invalid tokens, measured by authenticated and rejected test requests.\n51\t- Given a MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured, when a user exceeds the rate limit, then Limitador returns HTTP 429, measured by monitoring the response status codes.\n52\t- Structured log entries for authentication decisions and rate limit enforcement are emitted and retrievable from the CKS log aggregation, measured by querying Grafana Loki with label filters.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- HLR [P0]: Token rate limiting functional on xKS (Limitador deployment and enforcement).\n57\t- HLR [P0]: RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated (mechanism realization).\n58\t\n59\t## Gate Conditions\n60\t\n61\tExecuted only if the RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support investigation selects outcome B (no official RHCL xKS support; standalone deployment viable). If standalone deployment also fails on xKS, the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency branch applies.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b161464976813ea9","agent_id":"a92cfe628f6054f08","session_id":"1e4ce44d-83b4-4c13-8abf-b56141fd026a","uuid":"6567f61c-0499-4c5