{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aafd37754558fbc1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 13\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8003 Decomposition — RHAII MaaS on xKS (Dev Preview)\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below-threshold: the strategy is M-sized (3-5 sprints), spans multiple components (maas-api, maas-controller, Kuadrant Authorino/Limitador, Gateway API, observability stack) and three teams (MaaS, xKS Platform, RHCL/OpenShift), and contains multiple genuine unknowns explicitly flagged \"Needs validation\" whose answers change which epics exist or what they do. Not documentation-only: code, configuration, and Helm packaging changes are required.\n15\t\n16\t## Staff Engineer Input\n17\t\n18\tThe Staff Engineer Input section contains only template placeholder comments — no overrides to apply. Quotations attributed to staff engineers inside the AI-generated technical approach (CKS as the priority platform; observability as a core value proposition) are treated as strategy content and honored throughout: CKS-first validation ordering, P0 observability metrics, P1 logging, P2 tracing.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E001 | RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and deployment mechanism investigation | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E002 | MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability investigation | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E003 | maas-controller: configurable OIDC group claim mapping and Helm chart | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E004 | maas-api: xKS adaptation, external OIDC configuration, metrics instrumentation, Helm charts | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E005 | Kuadrant on xKS via official RHCL Helm charts (branch A) | Implementation (branch) | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E005 | Kuadrant on xKS via standalone Authorino + Limitador (branch B) | Implementation (branch) | MaaS | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-C-E005 | Auth enforcement via direct Envoy ext_authz integration (branch C) | Implementation (branch) | MaaS | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E006 | MaaS Dev Preview deployment validation on CKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E007 | MaaS Dev Preview deployment validation on AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E008 | MaaS observability: Perses dashboards and CKS Prometheus integration | Implementation | MaaS / xKS Platform | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E009 | MaaS observability: structured logging and CKS Loki integration | Implementation | MaaS / xKS Platform | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E010 | MaaS observability: distributed tracing via OpenTelemetry | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8003-E011 | MaaS on xKS documentation: user identity flow and known limitations | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n37\t\n38\tMain plan: 10 epics (E001-E004, E006-E011; the E005 slot is reserved for the branch-only Kuadrant deployment epics). Branch epics: 3 conditional alternatives attached to the E001 investigation. Total epic count: 13.\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support\"] --> E006\n45\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: MaaS 3.4 baseline changeability\"] --> E003\n46\t    E002 --> E004\n47\t    E003[\"E003 maas-controller group claim mapping\"] --> E006\n48\t    E004[\"E004 maas-api xKS adaptation\"] --> E006\n49\t    E004 --> E008\n50\t    E006[\"E006 CKS deployment validation\"] --> E007\n51\t    E006 --> E008\n52\t    E006 --> E009\n53\t    E006 --> E010\n54\t    E003 --> E011\n55\t    E004 --> E011\n56\t    E006 --> E011\n57\t    E007[\"E007 AKS deployment validation\"] --> E011\n58\t    E008[\"E008 metrics dashboards\"] --> E011\n59\t    E009[\"E009 structured logging\"] --> E011\n60\t    E010[\"E010 distributed tracing\"] --> E011\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\tBranch epics (not main-plan nodes): `BRANCH-A-E005`, `BRANCH-B-E005`, `BRANCH-C-E005` each depend on and are gated by E001. They are conditional alternatives — exactly one is executed based on the E001 outcome — and are attached to the E001 investigation as a plan document, not wired into the main-plan DAG.\n64\t\n65\t## Conditional Branches\n66\t\n67\tE001 (RHCL/Kuadrant xKS support and deployment mechanism) has three bounded outcomes, matching the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options:\n68\t\n69\t- **Branch A** — RHCL officially supports xKS with Helm charts: deploy Kuadrant via official RHCL Helm charts (owner: RHCL / OpenShift team).\n70\t- **Branch B** — No official RHCL xKS support: deploy standalone Authorino + Limitador via upstream Helm (owner: MaaS team).\n71\t- **Branch C** — Kuadrant cannot be installed on xKS at all: integrate auth enforcement directly via Envoy ext_authz, bypassing Kuadrant (owner: MaaS team; may require reworking MaaS AuthPolicy usage at the gateway).\n72\t\n73\tEach branch epic sets `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8003-E001` (also in its `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact`. Main-plan epics that need the outcome (E006) depend on the E001 investigation itself, never on a branch epic.\n74\t\n75\t## DAG Justification\n76\t\n77\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n78\t| --- | --- | --- |\n79\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | Whether the Tenant CRD is extensible in the 3.4 GA baseline determines whether the controller epic is a code change or a documentation fallback. |\n80\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | Whether maas-api can be instrumented without conflicting with the 3.4 GA freeze determines the metrics scope (in-place vs post-3.4 patch vs deferred). |\n81\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 (investigation gate, `rewrite`) | The selected Kuadrant deployment mechanism (branch A/B/C) determines what the CKS validation deploys and validates. |\n82\t| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Rule 3 / conditional branches | Three bounded deployment outcomes; exactly one branch epic is realized per the investigation result. |\n83\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 12/13 (build-against artifact) | CKS validation deploys and exercises the adapted maas-controller and its Helm chart. |\n84\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 12/13 (build-against artifact) | CKS validation deploys and exercises the adapted maas-api/payload-processing and their Helm charts. |\n85\t| E006 → E007 | Validation sequencing (strategy validation approach) | The strategy explicitly sequences CKS first, then AKS; AKS validation incorporates the CKS engineering delta and platform-specific GatewayClass options (envoy-gateway / azure-application-gateway). |\n86\t| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | Perses dashboards are built against the maas-api `/metrics` instrumentation (metric names and labels). |\n87\t| E006 → E008 | Serial (AC gate) | Dashboard acceptance requires live data from the CKS Prometheus instance scraping a deployed MaaS. |\n88\t| E006 → E009 | Serial (AC gate) | Logging acceptance requires a deployed MaaS emitting authentication, rate-limit, and validation events into CKS Grafana Loki. |\n89\t| E006 → E010 | Serial (AC gate) | Tracing acceptance requires the deployed request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend). |\n90\t| E003, E004, E006, E007, E008, E009, E010 → E011 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built and the limitations found across all implementation work. |\n91\t\n92\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n93\t\n94\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n95\t| --- | --- | --- |\n96\t| HLR-1: MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E006 |\n97\t| HLR-2: MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E007 |\n98\t| HLR-3: Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 (Limitador deployment), E006 (validation) |\n99\t| HLR-4: External OIDC authentication validated for user identity | P0 | E004 (configuration), E006 (validation) |\n100\t| HLR-5: API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E004 (confirmation of OIDC-agnostic path), E006 (validation) |\n101\t| HLR-6: Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E003 |\n102\t| HLR-7: RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and deployment mechanism validated | P0 | E001 (+ BRANCH-A/B/C-E005) |\n103\t| HLR-8: Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E004 (instrumentation), E008 (dashboards / Prometheus integration) |\n104\t| HLR-9: Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E003 (mapping), E006 (validation) |\n105\t| HLR-10: Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E009 |\n106\t| HLR-11: Known limitations documented | P1 | E011 (fed by E006/E007 findings) |\n107\t| HLR-12: User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E011 |\n108\t| HLR-13: Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E010 |\n109\t\n110\tAll 13 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n111\t\n112\t## Component Mappings\n113\t\n114\tCanonical component names are taken verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n115\t\n116\t| Strategy component | Canonical epic component | Reason |\n117\t| --- | --- | --- |\n118\t| Kuadrant (Authorino), Kuadrant (Limitador) | Model as a Service | No canonical Kuadrant component exists; the Kuadrant integration is documented inside the MaaS architecture context (`models-as-a-service.md`, `PLATFORM.md`) and exists solely to serve MaaS auth/rate-limiting on xKS. |\n119\t| Gateway API | (folded; no separate epic) | No canonical Gateway API component. The change is Helm chart values (configurable gateway class name instead of `openshift-default`) folded into E003/E004, plus platform validation inside E006/E007. The Gateway API implementation on CKS (Istio) is a confirmed precondition per the strategy's dependency table, not new component work. |\n120\t| Observability (Prometheus, Perses) | Monitoring | Closest canonical name for the Prometheus/Perses/Loki/Tempo stack; `odh-observability` exists in architecture context. |\n121\t| Documentation deliverables | Documentation | Canonical component for docs-authoring work. |\n122\t\n123\t## Health Warnings\n124\t\n125\t1. **Schedule risk (external, not an AC):** MaaS 3.4 GA stable build timing vs the Summit (May 2026) announcement. Mitigations per strategy: obtain RC build by April 27, run CKS validation in parallel, maintain a RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo. Captured as a risk on E006/E007, not as acceptance criteria.\n126\t2. **Scope trap — API key cache:** the risk table proposes an in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5 min) for validated API key hashes, but the strategy's failure-mode acceptance criteria expect API key validation to fail on PostgreSQL unavailability (cache-miss scenario). The cache is a mitigation option, not an HLR; the implementing team decides. Flagged to avoid silent scope growth in E004.\n127\t3. **Process gates pending human review:** ODH/RHOAI build onboarding (Helm chart build pipeline), license validation, and documentation support are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy's prerequisites table. No onboarding-chain epics were generated because both affected components (Model as a Service, Monitoring) already exist in architecture context; if build onboarding is later required for the Helm charts, it should be added as `konflux-onboarding`/`license-validation` epics.\n128\t4. **Docs priority exception:** E011 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority `Blocker` → Critical → P0) even though its HLRs are P1/P2. It does not push priority upstream.\n129\t5. **Potential scope gap:** the Impacted Teams table assigns xKS Platform \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\" (RHAII installer integration), but there is no affected-components row and no HLR for it; it is not decomposed into an epic. Product should confirm whether Dev Preview requires installer integration or standalone Helm deployment suffices.\n130\t6. **Open questions resolved without epics** (implementation details / schedule questions, per the two-way distinction): shared vs dedicated PostgreSQL (chart value decision, AC on E004); OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages (implementation detail, E004); latest stable MaaS 3.4 build date (PM schedule question — tracked in E002's findings and warning 1); CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility (validated as part of E006/E008/E009 acceptance, assumption confirmed by xKS Platform).\n131\t\n132\t## Tiered Delivery\n133\t\n134\tNot applied as a structural split. The external dependency with uncertain timing (MaaS 3.4 GA stable build) gates only the validation epics; all preparatory work (investigations, controller/api adaptation, Helm packaging, dashboards) is already schedulable in parallel and delivers risk-reduction value on an RC build. The strategy's own mitigation (RC build by April 27, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) is a schedule-risk handling, not a Tier 1/Tier 2 feature split.\n135\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8db71cbfae171a7a","agent_id":"a73443dcac864ec72","session_id":"1e4ce44d-83b4-4c13-8abf-b56141fd026a","uuid":"4cd9fa2a-6433-4025-9619-4fe909c838a7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:23:01.395Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aafd37754558fbc1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8003"}
